{
 "schema": "nebraskabeans.cultivars.v1",
 "what": "Growing degrees to flowering and to maturity for individual VARIETIES, measured on this project's own ground rather than assumed from the class.",
 "why": "The class model uses one number per market class. Measured, varieties inside a single class differ by up to 394 GDD \u2014 pinto runs from 1,808 (NE2-21-7) to 2,202 (91216-5) \u2014 while the gap BETWEEN classes in the shipped table is 55 GDD. Variety matters about seven times more than class, and flowering is worse: inside light red kidney, CELRK flowers at 833 GDD and L1032326 at 1,237, which are weeks apart. Flowering is when heat aborts pods, so staging the wrong variety puts the heat window in the wrong place entirely.",
 "source": {
  "name": "UNL Panhandle REEC, 2022 Nebraska Dry Edible Bean Variety Trials (EC3064), Urrea",
  "url": "https://extensionpubs.unl.edu/publication/2110/html/view",
  "sites": "Scottsbluff Ag Lab (planted 8 June 2022) and Mitchell Ag Lab (3 June), both irrigated",
  "api_key_required": false,
  "method": "UNL's own DTF and DTM \u2014 days to 50% flowering and to 80% of plants ready to harvest \u2014 converted to growing degrees base 50 F over the Alliance Municipal Airport ASOS daily record for 2022."
 },
 "limits": [
  "ONE SEASON, 2022, at two sites five miles apart. A variety's requirement is a physiological constant in principle; one year is not enough to publish it as one.",
  "These are the varieties UNL trialled, not the varieties actually planted in any field. Nothing here knows what a given grower planted \u2014 the satellite cannot see variety and USDA does not publish it. That is the gap this table cannot close on its own.",
  "Entries beginning NE, EX or a bare number are breeding lines, not commercial releases."
 ],
